course: dictionary
lesson: 6191
title: Miscount: when counting goes wrong
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: To miscount means to count wrongly, so the number you end with is not the right one. The little word mis- often means wrong, as in mistake or misread. «Bots read text in chunks, not letter by letter, and it was reported that some of them miscounted the r's in "strawberry", which has three.» The clue is "which has three": the bots got the number wrong.
explain: Miscount can also be a noun, meaning a wrong count. In an election, a miscount of the votes can lead to a recount, where the votes are counted again. In sport, a referee might miscount the players or the seconds.
explain: In maths, a common miscount is missing an object or counting one twice. To avoid this, touch or tick each object as you count it, and count in groups so you can keep track.
fact: To miscount means to count wrongly.
fact: As a noun, a miscount is a wrong count, especially of votes.
fact: «There was a miscount in the election» means the votes were counted wrongly.
fact: «Strawberry» has three r's, so counting only two r's is a miscount.
fact: Tip: touch or tick each object, and count in groups, to avoid a miscount.
exercise: «It was reported that some bots miscounted the r's in "strawberry", which has three.» What does miscounted mean here? | Spelled the word perfectly | Read the word backwards | Counted very quickly | *Counted wrongly
exercise: How many r's does "strawberry" have? | One | Four | *Three | Two
exercise: Sam counts the same apple twice. What has Sam done? | Shared the apples | Weighed the apples | Sorted the apples | *Miscounted
test: In an election, a miscount of the votes can lead to what? | A holiday | A new election song | Lunch | *A recount
test: In sport, a referee might miscount the what? | *Seconds | Flowers | Songs | Colours
exam: «There was a miscount in the election» uses miscount as which kind of word? | Adverb | *Noun | Verb | Adjective
